fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "The Reign of Quantity Inverted",
    "body": "Garton frames the episode around Rene Guenon's The Reign of Quantity and the Science of the Times, a book he had unpacked with Mario Garza two weeks earlier, proposing that the master inversion is quantity ranked over quality, with metaphysical essences condensing into matter rather than matter producing qualities out of itself. He borrows a guest's line that if everything is energy, we face an ignorance crisis rather than an energy crisis. Gober traces his own turn from Princeton, investment banking and Silicon Valley through a 2016 shift, and credits his late publisher Bill Gladstone with the title An End to Upside Down Thinking."
  },
  {
    "title": "Nobody Has the Reins",
    "body": "Gardner describes what he calls a personal cosmos in which, as he puts it, we are objectively impotent and subjectively omnipotent, pointing to outlier events such as September 11 and the airport screening that followed it, or the 2020 lockdowns, as evidence that the sequencing of events is not ours to set. He cites Ramana Maharshi's line that the only problem is identification, and a twenty year essay called On Surprises he plans to release on Substack. Gober answers with David Hawkins, the New York psychiatrist who wrote nine books and concluded that we are karmic windup toys let loose."
  },
  {
    "title": "Bodies That Keep the Record",
    "body": "Garton reads trauma in the biofield the way scorch marks read in the rings of tree stumps, saying collective events leave the same scratch in every record though the content stays personal. He recounts a client whose digestion he traced to broken trust with his mother and a Guatemala trip that cost him summer school, another whose chronic right ankle carried both a demand for perfection and the Book of Job's warning against it, and a teenage thigh injury that hardened into a belief that suffering is required to grow. He notes passionflower's lore of five stems for the five wounds of Christ, and that rest and stress are anagrams."
  },
  {
    "title": "Memory Wipes and Dream Initiations",
    "body": "On pre-birth planning Gober cites Robert Schwartz's Your Soul's Plan, the University of Virginia's two hundred plus cases of children with past life memories, birthmarks and deformities matched to medical records, and Barbara Bartolome, who told him on his 2019 series Where Is My Mind that she knew her purpose while near death and could not recall it afterward. Gardner rejects soul contracts on the grounds that we do not know what is best for us, and Slick Dissident's chat line calls soul contract theory paper terrorism. Garton describes a lucid dream of being reborn with his memories intact, waking in tears and accepting the forgetting."
  },
  {
    "title": "Ether, Scalar Fields and Proof",
    "body": "Gober argues that relativity rests on a reading of the 1887 Michelson Morley experiment which called a slight fringe shift a null result while treating a moving earth as settled, and he borrows Bernardo Kastrup's charge that time and space are language ghosts defined in circles. Gardner sets against that the standing columnar waves of turn of the century physics, Norberto Keppe's disinverted physics, and the bions of Wilhelm Reich and Viktor Schauberger, maintaining that the scalar domain holds limitless capacitance and that scarcity is a mathematical derivation. Garton closes on the replication crisis, Tom Cowan, Stephen Young's A Fool's Wisdom, and a listener's telescope footage of Mars."
  }
]

